WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.001 --> 00:00:03.891
There's a lot of nerves and excitement

00:00:03.891 --> 00:00:06.269
you gain quite a bit of speed 
in a short period of time.

00:00:06.269 --> 00:00:08.852
So you start kind of getting like 
tunnel vision almost.

00:00:08.852 --> 00:00:10.357
And then when you get into the flying

00:00:10.357 --> 00:00:13.929
 you see the whole mountainside just open up.

00:00:13.929 --> 00:00:19.736
My name is Anders Johnson and I am the 
Women's World Cup coach for USA Nordic.

00:00:19.736 --> 00:00:23.039
He's also a 3 time Olympian 
in the sport of ski jump

00:00:23.039 --> 00:00:28.512
a sport that's all about using technique in
physics to fly as far as humanly possible.

00:00:28.512 --> 00:00:32.884
And doing that successfully all comes down
to this shape.

00:00:38.085 --> 00:00:41.798
100 years ago, Olympic ski jumpers 
looked like this.

00:00:41.798 --> 00:00:44.058
Skis were held tightly in parallel.

00:00:44.058 --> 00:00:48.920
Hands in front of their face, kind of like 
a less posed version of Superman.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:52.948
By the 1950s, jumpers moved their arms back
and to the side.

00:00:52.948 --> 00:00:55.837
But they kept their skis in the same shape.

00:00:55.837 --> 00:00:57.658
Everyone did, because...

00:00:57.658 --> 00:01:01.400
The goal here is to fly farther
and with style.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:05.559
Farther because the further you land from
this line, the more points you get.

00:01:05.559 --> 00:01:08.390
And with style because judges award more points

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:11.713
on top of your distance score, 
just for looking cool.

00:01:13.922 --> 00:01:19.046
And for a while, the best, coolest, most stylish
way to ski jump was this.

00:01:19.046 --> 00:01:23.218
In a straight, elegant line, with skis tight
underneath you.

00:01:23.218 --> 00:01:26.899
This parallel position helped athletes cut
through the air — quickly.

00:01:26.899 --> 00:01:29.109
It was the standard for decades.

00:01:29.109 --> 00:01:31.650
That is, until the 1980s.

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:33.366
This Swedish guy named Jon Boklov.

00:01:33.366 --> 00:01:39.280
He struggled with keeping his skis parallel
but he noticed on some of the jumps

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.900
that he, you know, opened up his skis a little
bit wider into a bigger v

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.440
he was achieving longer distances.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:49.822
That’s because this position allows you
to catch a lot more air with your body 

00:01:49.822 --> 00:01:51.460
than this one.

00:01:52.149 --> 00:01:55.260
You have kind of two key parameters 
you're trying to optimize:

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:58.793
how fast you're moving forward but also 
how fast you're moving down.

00:01:58.793 --> 00:02:02.069
Tess Saxton-Fox is an 
assistant aerodynamics professor

00:02:02.069 --> 00:02:04.895
at the University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign.

00:02:04.895 --> 00:02:08.569
My research is on the flow of air really close
to the surface of planes.

00:02:08.569 --> 00:02:11.370
Air flow is key to ski jumping to because...

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:14.490
You want to go down as slowly as possible
once you're in the air

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:15.675
because you want to stay aloft

00:02:15.675 --> 00:02:17.496
but you want to go forward 
as fast as you can.

00:02:17.496 --> 00:02:19.840
Surface area has a lot of power there.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:22.630
The parallel position has fairly low 
surface area.

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:25.780
It makes the athlete as thin and small 
as possible.

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:27.880
This is actually a pretty good 
aerodynamic shape

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:30.646
if you're just trying to maximize 
your forward speed.

00:02:30.646 --> 00:02:32.910
You are lining up your body with the skis

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:36.140
and so the air can go around the ski 
and the body at the same time

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:39.434
in kind of the same little bubble that it
forms around you.

00:02:39.434 --> 00:02:41.609
And so that's good actually for reducing drag.

00:02:41.609 --> 00:02:44.436
What its not good at is resisting gravity.

00:02:44.436 --> 00:02:48.000
It doesn't do much to prevent you from 
falling through the air to the ground.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.384
In contrast, when athletes open their skis
up into a V

00:02:51.384 --> 00:02:56.212
they’re maximizing their surface area by allowing
air to directly push against their body.

00:02:56.212 --> 00:03:00.287
Instead of just trying to be like a bullet,
you're trying to be a wing.

00:03:00.287 --> 00:03:03.313
This position helps create something called
“lift.”

00:03:03.313 --> 00:03:05.207
Yeah, lift is a tricky concept.

00:03:05.207 --> 00:03:09.298
And it's an important concept, it's the basis
for, you know, flight.

00:03:09.298 --> 00:03:11.638
It’s what makes planes fly, 
what makes birds fly.

00:03:11.638 --> 00:03:15.786
The key is that as the air kind of 
moves over the body

00:03:15.786 --> 00:03:17.775
it kind of follows the shape of the body.

00:03:17.775 --> 00:03:19.380
So you'll see that they're at an angle.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:20.950
And that's actually really important.

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:24.000
If you put your hand out the window of a car
and you put it straight

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.175
it just pushes straight backwards.

00:03:25.175 --> 00:03:28.390
But if you put it on an angle, you can kind
of play with it, right?

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:31.445
It'll actually push you up, push your hand
up and back.

00:03:31.445 --> 00:03:32.680
That's really what they're doing is

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:36.430
they're putting themselves at an angle so
that they get a force

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:39.973
that actually wants to push them up as well
as backwards.

00:03:39.973 --> 00:03:43.319
This 1995 paper from the 
Journal of Applied Biomechanics

00:03:43.319 --> 00:03:46.069
does a great job of showing this graphically.

00:03:46.069 --> 00:03:50.053
The researchers used computer simulations
to test out different ski styles.

00:03:50.053 --> 00:03:53.750
Most importantly the parallel style, which
they refer to as classic style

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:55.442
and the V-style.

00:03:55.442 --> 00:03:57.862
The V-style generated much more lift.

00:03:57.862 --> 00:04:01.712
This extra lift means that athletes 
got more air time

00:04:01.712 --> 00:04:07.183
which in turn allows further distances 
and, ultimately, more points.

00:04:09.501 --> 00:04:12.270
When Jan Boklov first implemented the V-style

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:14.755
it was ridiculed it for its ugly appearance.

00:04:14.755 --> 00:04:17.500
The Chicago Tribune described the switch 
as going from

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:19.440
an “elegantly parallel position”

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:23.464
to “resembling puppies leaping 
into a swimming pool.”

00:04:23.464 --> 00:04:25.789
And Jan lost style points because of it.

00:04:25.789 --> 00:04:27.891
"Here's the man with a unique style."

00:04:27.891 --> 00:04:34.559
While he travelled 78 meters in this 1988
jump, he only got 85.4 points.

00:04:34.559 --> 00:04:37.599
Denis McGrane of the US travelled 
the same distance

00:04:37.599 --> 00:04:39.416
but he used the classic method.

00:04:39.416 --> 00:04:42.141
He got 90.4 points.

00:04:42.141 --> 00:04:47.181
Somewhere along the lines in the early 90s,
people started to notice that it was effective.

00:04:47.181 --> 00:04:51.598
Other teams and other athletes kind of started
to test it a little bit.

00:04:51.598 --> 00:04:54.800
By the 1992 winter Olympics, 
the gold medal was given to

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:59.596
Finnish skier Toni Nieminen, a 16-year-old
who used the V-style.

00:04:59.596 --> 00:05:05.165
He traveled 122 meters, beating every other
athlete on distance.

00:05:05.165 --> 00:05:10.125
And as more gold medals went to athletes who
used the V-style, the old style faded away.

00:05:10.125 --> 00:05:15.196
It sort of just became, you know, probably
one of the biggest progressions in the sport.

00:05:15.196 --> 00:05:18.976
Today, you’d be hard pressed to find an
athlete who doesn’t use V-style 

00:05:18.976 --> 00:05:20.555
or a variation on it.

00:05:20.555 --> 00:05:24.552
But that doesn’t mean that there isn’t
still room to innovate on body shape.

00:05:24.552 --> 00:05:28.800
One thing that I would be curious about 
is doing a V the other way.

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:32.247
So having the tips more closed and the back
more open.

00:05:32.247 --> 00:05:35.767
There's a lot of kind of research on the stability
of having that kind of a triangle shape.

00:05:35.767 --> 00:05:38.817
I would think it would be a very stable configuration.

